explanation.md:
```c
struct Node {
    int data;          /* the value */
    struct Node *next; /* the address of the node after this one */
};
```

Two things to notice.

1. The struct contains a **pointer to its own type**. That is legal in C — the `struct Node` tag is known inside its own definition, so `struct Node *next` compiles. (In C++ you would write `Node*`.)
2. `next` is a **pointer**, so it holds an *address*, not a value. `next->data` is the value stored in the node that this pointer points at.

Why not an array of values plus an array of indices? Because the whole point is that the nodes do not have to sit next to each other in memory. Each `malloc` hands back whatever address happens to be free.

Walking a list is one rule: **keep a pointer to the current node, follow `next`, stop at `NULL`.**

```c
struct Node *cursor = head;
while (cursor != NULL) {
    printf("%d\n", cursor->data);
    cursor = cursor->next;
}
```

Two things beginners get wrong:

- **Using `head` as the walking pointer.** If you overwrite `head`, you destroy the list's identity and can never walk it again. Use a separate cursor.
- **Forgetting that `cursor` is a copy of an address.** `cursor = cursor->next` does not change the list at all — it only moves your bookmark. That is exactly why it is safe.

Every traversal is **O(n)** — you must visit every node. So searching is O(n) too, because a linked list has no index.

Two implementations, the same four operations.

**On an array**, the stack is the array plus one index, `top`, holding the index of the top element (`-1` when the stack is empty):

```c
int stack[100];
int top = -1;

push(x): top = top + 1;  stack[top] = x;
pop():   x = stack[top]; top = top - 1;  return x;
```

Each operation is one increment and one array write. **O(1).** The limit is the array size — when `top` runs past the end you have overflow, and the usual fixes are a bigger array or a growable one.

**On a linked list**, the stack is the list with `top` pointing at the head:

```c
push(x): temp->next = top;  top = temp;
pop():   temp = top;  top = top->next;  free(temp);  return temp->data;
```

Also O(1) — but only because we push and pop at the **head**. Push at the tail and pop at the tail is O(n), because finding the tail means walking the list. The rule for the whole topic: **the end you work at decides the cost.**

